WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.567
-I want to talk to you about so many things.

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:03.801
I want to talk about "King Richard."

00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:05.067
I want to talk to you about this memoir.

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:06.501
-Yes. -"Will."

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:09.801
It is named "Will," because obviously, your name is Will,

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:15.467
but also, it shows every high, every low, and the sheer will

00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:16.834
that it took you to become who you are.

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:18.634
-Yes, sir. -I love the name.

00:00:18.634 --> 00:00:20.501
And I love the book. -Thank you, man.

00:00:20.501 --> 00:00:23.100
-This is -- Did you know you were going to --

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.534
you were writing this when you started writing this?

00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:29.100
-You know, it was -- you never know what it's gonna be.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.934
This is my first time -- I've never, you know,

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:34.033
written any -- I've written music,

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.067
but I've never written a book before.

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.300
So it was the first time. So it's terrifying.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.901
And, you know, I worked with Mark Manson,

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:43.734
and he did a really great job of guiding me through this process.

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:46.334
-It's fantastic. It's so good.

00:00:46.334 --> 00:00:48.467
-I bled on that thing right there, man.

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.701
-You really did, bud. I mean, you told every story.

00:00:51.701 --> 00:00:53.300
-In Philly, we called that

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.667
"putting your business in the street."

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.000
[ Laughter ]

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.567
-You did, right? That's --

00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:58.801
You definitely did. I mean --

00:00:58.801 --> 00:01:00.267
I mean, you talk about everything.

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:03.834
You talk about relationships and the career in Hollywood.

00:01:03.834 --> 00:01:06.467
And then, you talk a lot about your dad.

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:07.868
-Yeah, yeah. -Which I didn't know,

00:01:07.868 --> 00:01:10.234
which is -- is it --

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:12.267
Does it feel odd to talk about it now?

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:14.067
-You know, that was a big part

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:17.868
of getting the freedom to talk about it.

00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:21.434
My father passed in 2016. -Wow.

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:25.234
-You know, and somehow, after he passed, I got free.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:26.767
You know, there were things I just never would have

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:29.868
talked about from my childhood, you know, while he was alive.

00:01:29.868 --> 00:01:31.634
My father was, you know,

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:34.567
the greatest man I've ever known, you know?

00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:36.934
But he also had some issues.

00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:40.133
It was really a double-edged sword.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:44.801
And, you know, just those experiences

00:01:44.801 --> 00:01:49.701
didn't fit into the -- the narrative of Will Smith.

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:51.801
You know? So it was, you know,

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:55.100
after he passed, and, you know, I guess I felt like

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.033
I had suffered enough in my life that I could give advice.

00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:00.634
You know? It feels like you can't --

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:03.801
you know, you can't give advice until you've earned it.

00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:05.033
-Yeah. -You know?

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:06.934
-I just felt like I was in a place in my life

00:02:06.934 --> 00:02:11.801
where I had reflected enough and I had healed enough things,

00:02:11.801 --> 00:02:15.767
I had experienced enough things that I felt that it would

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:18.267
have some value of what I was saying.

00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:19.734
-You couldn't have written this 10 years ago.

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:21.934
-No, no, not at all. Not at all.

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:26.934
Just the amount of, you know, reflection on my life --

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:29.334
And even in the process of reading, you know?

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:30.801
I mean, in the process of writing,

00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:34.234
it's really great to actually have to go back through

00:02:34.234 --> 00:02:37.367
and look at your life, and theres so many things

00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:40.901
that we all have that we refuse to look at,

00:02:40.901 --> 00:02:42.267
we refuse to talk about.

00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:44.467
Our family acts like it never happened.

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:46.634
You know. -Trust me, I'm Irish.

00:02:46.634 --> 00:02:48.734
Yeah, yeah. -Yeah, yeah.

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:50.567
-Thanksgiving, like, "Hey, we're cool, right?"

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:52.167
-"Hey, Jimmy, what's that under the rug?"

00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:54.534
-"Nothing! Nothing." [ Laughter ]

00:02:54.534 --> 00:02:56.701
"Just leave that there. Put your drink on it."

00:02:56.701 --> 00:02:58.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:00.601
-Exactly right. Yeah.

00:03:00.601 --> 00:03:02.934
But, yeah, once you -- I guess once you do it --

00:03:02.934 --> 00:03:08.300
-But it is so liberating. It is so liberating to be able

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:14.133
to just, you know, put down the -- the character

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:16.968
and to be able to go out into the world

00:03:16.968 --> 00:03:21.000
and just live authentically as you are, you know?

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.567
-I think it makes you, too, it makes you enjoy what you did

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:27.133
even more to know what struggles you've been through.

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:29.701
Everyone has a story. -Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:29.701 --> 00:03:32.567
-Everybody does. And so, you go, "Oh, man.

00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:33.901
You must have had a blast if you were doing that."

00:03:33.901 --> 00:03:36.868
And you go, "Yeah, I did. I had fun."

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.133
Obviously, you talk about the first time

00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:39.234
you heard your song on the radio.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:40.701
-Yes, exactly. -It's those moments,

00:03:40.701 --> 00:03:42.300
you're like -- I mean, I've never --

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.667
we've never experienced -- Well, The Roots have.

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.868
But I have not experienced my song on the radio.

00:03:46.868 --> 00:03:48.434
-Yeah. -What does that feel like?

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:51.100
-You know, the thing, you hear your song on the radio

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.601
for the first time -- Because you have heard it

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:54.534
yourself, you know, a thousand times.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.868
-Yeah. -And then, the idea --

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.067
I remember I was in my father's shop.

00:03:58.067 --> 00:03:59.534
And it was a record called

00:03:59.534 --> 00:04:00.868
"Girls Ain't Nothing But Trouble."

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:02.200
It was our first record.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.200
And I remember seeing a guy go passed in his car,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.133
listening to the record.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:09.934
I heard it on the radio because it was

00:04:09.934 --> 00:04:13.767
on my station inside, and it was like it's in the world.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:15.133
-Yeah. -You know?

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:17.367
It's in the world now. And it's, like --

00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:21.434
It is such a -- a beautiful feeling.

00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:23.000
But, you know, I talked about in the book.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.868
What happened is, I had told my mother --

00:04:25.868 --> 00:04:28.000
My mother graduated from Carnegie Mellon,

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.801
and she was dead serious about education.

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:32.334
And in her mind,

00:04:32.334 --> 00:04:35.567
if you didn't have an education, you were dead.

00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:37.868
-Yeah. You did it wrong.

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:40.634
-You can't survive in this world without an education.

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:42.634
You know, so when I told my mother

00:04:42.634 --> 00:04:45.634
that I wasn't going to college, it was like the worst thing

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:48.434
that I ever -- 'Cause I was going to do

00:04:48.434 --> 00:04:50.400
"hippity hop," you know? [ Laughter ]

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.834
I was gonna do hippity hop. My mother, she worked

00:04:52.834 --> 00:04:54.400
for the school board of Philadelphia,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.501
and we couldn't say "ain't" in our house, you know?

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.367
I remember one time, I was running out,

00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:02.634
and I screamed to my friends,

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:05.534
"Yo, where y'all gon' be at?!" And my mother said, "Unh-unh.

00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:08.067
Hopefully they gonna be behind that preposition."

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:12.968
You know? [ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:12.968 --> 00:05:16.167
-That's exactly right. -You know, when I told her

00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:18.968
I wasn't going to college because I wanted to rap,

00:05:18.968 --> 00:05:20.300
you know, it was real -- -She doesn't know

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.033
you're gonna be will Smith. -Yeah, yeah.

00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:27.634
She had no idea. So my father let me have a year.

00:05:27.634 --> 00:05:31.501
So, I had a year that I could go try to do rap music, and then,

00:05:31.501 --> 00:05:33.467
after that, I was gonna have to go to college.

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:36.667
And within that year, we won the first Grammy ever given

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:39.234
to a rap artist, you know? [ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:39.234 --> 00:05:41.567
-That's how you do it, man. -I know.

00:05:41.567 --> 00:05:42.701
-I love it! -Yeah.

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:46.834
-Honestly, it's awesome. -"Take that, Mom!"

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:48.067
-No, no, no! That's not the point.

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:49.701
-Oh, I thought that's what you were saying.

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:52.801
-That's not the point. But I want to --

00:05:52.801 --> 00:05:57.734
I saw a clip of you talking with Oprah, and she said,

00:05:57.734 --> 00:06:00.100
"This is the best memoir, or the greatest memoir,

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.300
she's ever read." -Yes.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.367
She said in her life, too. -Yeah.

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:06.133
[ Laughter and applause ] -Thank you.

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:08.300
Don't be humble. -No, no, no.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.234
You know, I'm just saying, if you're gonna quote

00:06:10.234 --> 00:06:14.334
Miss Oprah, quote her correctly. [ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:16.701
-That's a big deal, man.

00:06:16.701 --> 00:06:20.067
That's a big deal, Will, to have Oprah Winfrey say that.

00:06:20.067 --> 00:06:23.367
-Yeah. No, when Oprah -- Oprah is, like, the book lady

00:06:23.367 --> 00:06:28.400
of the world, so when Oprah likes your book, people listen.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.000
-And she doesn't make it up, either.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.501
She wouldn't say that. -She would never say that.

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:34.334
You know? She had me crying.

00:06:34.334 --> 00:06:35.534
You know? She made me cry.

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:37.801
-She's good like that. She's a pro.

00:06:37.801 --> 00:06:40.200
-And I saw my book, and she had it marked up.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.901
She literally had -- -I saw the post-it notes.

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:44.501
-All the post-it notes inside the book.

00:06:44.501 --> 00:06:46.334
And it was -- you know...

00:06:46.334 --> 00:06:48.100
-It's a little different than my book.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.400
-Yeah. -I took all mine out.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.834
-I took all the post-it notes out.

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:52.767
-Because you didn't like how it would look on camera,

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:54.467
to see all the post-it notes. -Exactly.

00:06:54.467 --> 00:06:56.167
-You thought it was unprofessional for Miss Oprah

00:06:56.167 --> 00:06:58.567
to have all those post-it notes on camera.

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:00.200
-That's why I took it out of my book.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.300
-What was your favorite part of the book, Jimmy?

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.667
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:06.033
[ Drum roll ]

00:07:06.033 --> 00:07:07.601
Uh-huh. Uh-huh.

00:07:07.601 --> 00:07:09.934
-Downloading it on Audible.

00:07:09.934 --> 00:07:11.734
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.734 --> 00:07:13.934
It's the best! It's so good.

00:07:13.934 --> 00:07:15.701
Oh, my God. When we come back,

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.634
I want to talk about your new movie, "King Richard."

00:07:17.634 --> 00:07:19.300
More with Will Smith, everybody.

